  "answer": "### If the fee is not paid\n**Manual practice:** Part 4 says that an act is not done (or is taken not to have been done), a document is taken not to be filed, and an application for trade mark registration is taken to have lapsed if the required fee has not been paid. It requires written notification within 14 days. [TMM/Part4/1/1/3/1]\n\nPart 2 says that, if notification of underpayment is not given within **14 working days**, the application, notice or request is treated as filed, but the unpaid amount remains a debt to the Commonwealth. The Registrar must not perform an act attracting a fee, such as examination, until that fee is paid. [TMM/Part2/4/part-2-4-3-fee-not-paid-or-underpaid]\n\n**Uncertainty:** the supplied Manual passages differ between “14 days” and “14 working days”. The relevant legislative provisions are not supplied, so I cannot resolve that discrepancy from these sources. [TMM/Part4/1/1/3/1; TMM/Part2/4/part-2-4-3-fee-not-paid-or-underpaid]\n\n### Can the fee be refunded?\n**Manual practice:** yes, in specified circumstances—not as an unrestricted entitlement. These include overpayment, documents taken not to have been filed, duplicate payments, officer error and inapplicable payments. Appropriate delegate consideration and approval are required where necessary. [TMM/Part4/5#1]\n\nFor an underpayment notified within the stated 14-day period, Part 4 says the act or document remains ineffective until the deficiency is made good; if it is not made good, fees already paid will be refunded after two months. [TMM/Part4/2/2/5]\n\nThe Manual also says the Registrar may remit all or part of an unpaid fee, or refund all or part of a paid fee, where liability arose through a trade marks officer’s error or omission. [TMM/Part4/2/2/1]\n\n**Regulations:** the supplied extract of regulation 21.24(1) identifies liability arising from an employee’s error or omission, but ends at “the Registrar may:”. It does not supply the operative refund or remission powers; those details above are therefore reported as Manual practice, not as a complete statement of the Regulations. [TMR1995/r21.24(1)]"
